Blind SQL Injection with Time Delays and Information Retrieval

Introduction
One of the biggest misconceptions about SQL injection is that successful exploitation always returns database errors or sensitive data on the screen.
This lab challenged that assumption. The application behaved almost identically regardless of the payload I sent. There were no error messages, no SQL output, and no obvious indication that my input was being interpreted by the database.
The only observable difference was 'time'.
That single characteristic became the foundation of the entire attack.

Understanding the Challenge
Blind SQL injection forces you to stop looking for visible responses and instead observe how the application behaves.
Rather than asking, 'What data did the database return?', the real question becomes:
-Did the database behave differently because my condition evaluated to TRUE?
In this lab, a delayed response was the only confirmation available.

My Approach
I started by verifying whether the vulnerable parameter could influence the SQL query.
Instead of attempting to retrieve data immediately, I injected a conditional statement designed to pause the database only when a specific condition was satisfied.
When the application consistently responded several seconds later, it confirmed two important observations:
My injected SQL was being executed.
-Response time could be used as a reliable source of information.
At that point, the application had effectively become a binary communication channel.
Every request produced one of two outcomes:
-Delayed response → TRUE
-Immediate response → FALSE

Although simple in concept, repeating this process methodically allowed hidden data to be reconstructed without ever displaying it directly.

Payload Example
sql
';SELECT CASE
WHEN (SUBSTRING(password,1,1)='a')
THEN pg_sleep(10)
ELSE pg_sleep(0)
END
FROM users
WHERE username='administrator'--

Rather than exposing the first character of the password, this payload simply asks the database whether the first character is 'a'.
If the answer is yes, execution pauses.
If the answer is no, the application responds normally.
Repeating this logic for every position and every possible character eventually reveals the complete password.

The technical aspect of this lab wasn't especially difficult.
The real challenge was maintaining a structured approach throughout the extraction process. Because no information is displayed directly, every conclusion depends on careful observation and consistency. A single incorrect assumption early in the process can affect everything that follows.

Security Impact
Time-based blind SQL injection demonstrates that suppressing database errors does not eliminate SQL injection risks.
An attacker may still be able to:
-Enumerate database objects.
-Recover usernames and passwords.
-Extract confidential business information.
-Compromise privileged accounts.
-Pivot to further attacks after gaining valid credentials.

The absence of visible output should never be considered evidence that an application is secure.

 Mitigation
The most effective defenses include:

-Using parameterized queries for every database interaction.
-Validating and sanitizing user-controlled input.
-Applying least-privilege permissions to database accounts.
-Monitoring abnormal response patterns that may indicate automated extraction attempts.
-Conducting regular security assessments to identify injection flaws before deployment.

